+# Design - Make the shortener enterprise-ready and faster
+
+Requirement: **REQ-AMBIGUOUS** (v2, risk high)
+
+## Summary
+
+Make the shortener enterprise-ready and faster. Update from security review: enterprise-ready requires API-key authentication on all write endpoints (create, update, delete). Keys must be stored hashed and only the owning key may modify or delete a link. Impact analysis: 5 of 26 modules impacted, 2 routes, blast radius 0.18.
+
+## Components
+
+- service.cache - Cache protocol, bounded in-memory TTL LRU, Redis backend
+- service.ratelimit - token bucket (in-memory, Redis Lua for atomicity)
+- service.metrics - per-app registry, request/redirect/cache/rate-limit/analytics metrics, /metrics
+- scripts/loadcheck.py - starts uvicorn, seeds links, measures p50/p95 redirect latency
+- service.auth - key generation (usk_<prefix>_<secret>), SHA-256 at rest, constant-time verify, require_api_key dependency
+- service.cli - create/revoke keys
+
+## Decisions
+
+| Decision | Rationale / rejected alternative |
+|---|---|
+| Read-through cache of the resolved link (positive + short negative TTL) | removes the DB from the hot path; negative caching blunts enumeration |
+| Invalidate on PATCH/DELETE | correctness over hit rate for state changes |
+| Per API key for writes, per client IP for redirects | fairness for authenticated clients, abuse protection for public traffic |
+| 429 with Retry-After and X-RateLimit-* headers | standard, client-friendly back-off signal |
+| Per-app CollectorRegistry | isolated in tests, no global state |
+| p95 < 50 ms @ 20 concurrent clients (laptop, SQLite) enforced as a gate | a concrete, repeatable number replaces 'faster' |
+| Opaque random keys hashed with SHA-256 (keys have 256 bits of entropy, so a slow KDF adds nothing) | fast verification on every write without storing secrets |
+| Owner-only PATCH/DELETE | prevents one tenant modifying another tenant's links |
+
+## Data model
+
+- `cache key link:{code} -> {target, gone, expires_at}`
+- `bucket state {tokens, updated_at} per key`
+- `api_keys(id, name, prefix UNIQUE, key_hash, created_at, revoked_at); links.owner_key_id FK NULL`
+
+## Risks and assumptions
+
+- [enterprise-ready] Interpret as read-through caching of redirects, per-client rate limiting and Prometheus metrics. Authentication changes the public API contract, so it is NOT assumed; it is raised as open question Q-1.
+- [faster] Target the redirect hot path - p95 latency under 50 ms with 20 concurrent clients on a developer laptop (SQLite), measured by scripts/loadcheck.py and enforced as a gate.
+
+## Acceptance criteria
+
+- **AC-C1** Given a link that was resolved once, when it is followed again, then the redirect is served from cache without a database lookup.
+- **AC-C2** Given a cached link, when it is disabled or deleted, then the next redirect reflects the change.
+- **AC-R1** Given a client over its rate limit, when it sends another request, then the API returns 429 with Retry-After.
+- **AC-O1** Given traffic has been served, when Prometheus scrapes /metrics, then request, redirect and cache metrics are exposed.
+- **AC-P1** Given 20 concurrent clients, when they follow links, then p95 redirect latency is under 50 ms.
+- **AC-A1** Given no or an invalid X-API-Key, when a client calls a write endpoint, then the API returns 401.
+- **AC-A2** Given a link created by key A, when key B tries to modify or delete it, then the API returns 403.
+- **AC-A3** Given an API key, when it is stored, then only its SHA-256 hash is persisted.
+
+## Open questions
+
+- Q-2: Is there a production SLO (p95/p99, RPS) that should replace the laptop target?
